<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick Douglass</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1818–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Frederick Augustus Washington Bailey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick Douglass was an American abolitionist, writer and statesman who escaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> in 1838 and became the most influential African American of the nineteenth century. His Narrative of 1845 made him famous on both sides of the Atlantic, and his newspaper and speeches drove the campaign against slavery. During the Civil War he urged the enlistment of Black soldiers, and afterwards he served as a federal official and minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> while campaigning for civil and women's rights.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-douglass.jpg" alt="Frederick Douglass" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Kendall Warren · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frederick_Douglass_(circa_1879)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick Augustus Washington Bailey was born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in Talbot County, on the Eastern Shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>. His mother, Harriet Bailey, was enslaved, and his father, who may have been her master, was apparently white; Douglass later wrote that he searched in vain for his father's name. He did not know his age. Records of his former owner, Aaron Anthony, led the historian Dickson J. Preston to place his birth in February 1818, and Douglass chose to celebrate 14 February.</p><p>At six he was moved to the Wye House plantation. After Anthony's death he was sent to Baltimore, to Hugh and Sophia Auld, and in 1835 he was hired out as a ship caulker, where white workers who feared competition from enslaved labour abused him. In 1837 he met Anna Murray, a free Black woman, who encouraged him and gave him money. He escaped to the North in September 1838 and settled in New Bedford, Massachusetts, taking the surname Douglass on the suggestion of Nathan Johnson, who had read Walter Scott's The Lady of the Lake.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Douglass became a leader of the abolitionist movement in Massachusetts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. His Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave (1845) was an immediate bestseller, reprinted nine times in three years. Fearing that publicity would draw the attention of his former owner, friends sent him to tour Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from August 1845. On his return in 1847, with £500 from English supporters, he began the North Star in Rochester, New York, which he later merged into Frederick Douglass' Paper.</p><p>In 1848 he was the only Black person at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention">Seneca Falls Convention</a>, where he spoke for women's suffrage. During the Civil War he argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> should fight, published Men of Color to Arms! in March 1863, and two of his sons joined the 54th Massachusetts Infantry Regiment. After the war he campaigned for the rights of freed people, became president of the Freedman's Savings Bank in 1874 shortly before its failure, and in 1872 was nominated, without his knowledge, as vice president on the Equal Rights Party ticket.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Douglass wrote two further autobiographies, My Bondage and My Freedom (1855) and Life and Times of Frederick Douglass (1881, revised 1892). On 20 February 1895 he attended a meeting of the National Council of Women in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, received a standing ovation and died of a heart attack shortly after returning home. His funeral was held at the Metropolitan African Methodist Episcopal Church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick Douglass” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lord John Russell, Napoleon III and Gladstone toyed with recognition or mediation until Antietam and the preliminary Emancipation Proclamation of September 1862 ended it; British opinion, proud of ending the slave trade and taught by <b>Frederick Douglass</b> and other Black abolitionists, was against the Confederacy except in Liverpool.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…made the case in their own words: Ottobah Cugoano in 1787, Olaudah Equiano in his Interesting Narrative of 1789, Mary Prince, whose History of 1831 was the first account of the life of an enslaved Black woman published in Britain, and <b>Frederick Douglass</b> in his Narrative of 1845.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>About 300 people attended and 100, 68 women and 32 men, signed the Declaration of Sentiments; the resolution on the vote, the most radical, passed only after the abolitionist <b>Frederick Douglass</b> spoke for it.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick Douglass</b>, Brown's friend and admirer, wrote in 1874 that Brown had been with the troops throughout the war, in every camp fire, as they marched on to victory and freedom.</blockquote><a href="/songs/john-browns-body">John Brown's Body</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick Douglass</b>, in his speech &quot;What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?&quot;, compared being asked to speak on the Fourth of July to the exiles of the psalm being asked to sing in a foreign land.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-137">Psalm 137</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick Douglass</b> wrote that the songs he heard in slavery were &quot;a testimony against slavery, and a prayer to God for deliverance from chains&quot;, and W. E. B. Du Bois, in The Souls of Black Folk (1903), called these &quot;Sorrow Songs&quot; the &quot;singular spiritual heritage of the nation&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wade-in-the-water">Wade in the Water</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Talbot County, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.75,-76.18&amp;year=1868" aria-label="Talbot County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2660 773 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick Douglass"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3110 1043h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3110 1043h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.75,-76.18&amp;year=1868">What was here in 1868? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You have seen how a man was made a slave; you shall see how a slave was made a man.&#8221;</p><footer>Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave (1845) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23/pg23-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;From that moment, I understood the pathway from slavery to freedom.&#8221;</p><footer>Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass, an American Slave (1845), on learning to read &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23/pg23-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For it is not light that is needed, but fire; it is not the gentle shower, but thunder. We need the storm, the whirlwind, and the earthquake.&#8221;</p><footer>What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?, speech at Rochester (1852) &#183; original &#183; as printed in My Bondage and My Freedom (1855) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/202/pg202-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You are loosed from your moorings, and are free; I am fast in my chains, and am a slave! You move merrily before the gentle gale, and I sadly before the bloody whip!&#8221;</p><footer>Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass (1845), chapter 10, to the ships on Chesapeake Bay &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23/pg23-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What to the American slave is your Fourth of July? I answer, a day that reveals to him, more than all other days in the year, the gross injustice and cruelty to which he is the constant victim.&#8221;</p><footer>What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?, speech at Rochester (1852) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/202/pg202-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A man, without force, is without the essential dignity of humanity. Human nature is so constituted, that it cannot honor a helpless man, although it can pity him…&#8221;</p><footer>My Bondage and My Freedom (1855), on his fight with the slave-breaker Covey &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/202/pg202-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
